---
import Footer from '../components/landing/Footer.astro';
import Nav from '../components/landing/Nav.astro';
import Landing from '../layouts/Landing.astro';
import { DMG, RELEASES } from '../lib/downloads';
import '../styles/page.css';

const repo = 'https://github.com/wsoule/dispatch';
---

<Landing
  title="Docs: set up Dispatch and land your first agent change"
  description="Download Dispatch, follow Setup, and land your first change with an AI agent. The terminal path follows."
  path="/docs"
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <h1>Quickstart</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          From download to an agent landing its first change in a repo you already have, without
          opening a terminal. The <a href={`${repo}#readme`}>README</a> goes deeper on every step.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="get-started">Get started</h2>

        <h3>1. Download</h3>
        <p>
          Download Dispatch for Mac, <a href={DMG.arm}>Apple Silicon</a> or <a href={DMG.x64}>Intel</a>,
          open the DMG and drag Dispatch into Applications. Builds are signed and notarized. Linux
          packages (<code>.deb</code>, <code>.rpm</code>, <code>.AppImage</code>) are on the{' '}
          <a href={RELEASES}>latest release</a>. Or, with Homebrew:
        </p>
        <pre><code>brew install --cask wsoule/tap/dispatch</code></pre>

        <h3>2. Open it and follow Setup</h3>
        <p>Setup checks three things and fixes each with a button. Anything already in place shows a tick.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Git.</strong> On a Mac without it, Setup opens Apple's installer.</li>
          <li><strong>Your project.</strong> Choose a folder or clone one from GitHub. A folder without version tracking gets it turned on, with a first snapshot.</li>
          <li><strong>Your AI agent.</strong> Pick Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI or another supported agent. Setup installs it and opens its own sign-in, so it runs on the plan you already have. An API key works too.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>3. Land your first change</h3>
        <p>
          Describe a change in a sentence. A short guide walks you through it: check the task, send
          it to the agent, review what changed, and add it to your project. The agent works on its
          own copy of your project, so nothing changes in your files until you say so.
        </p>

        <h2 id="terminal">From the terminal</h2>
        <p>
          The <code>dispatch</code> command comes with the Homebrew install. After a DMG or Linux
          install, add it from <strong>Settings › Setup › Terminal command</strong>. Then, in any git
          repository:
        </p>
        <pre><code>dispatch init
dispatch task create "My first task" --priority high
dispatch task list</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>dispatch init</code> creates <code>.dispatch/</code> with the project's settings. Tasks
          are markdown files in <code>.dispatch/tasks/</code> by default, or, with{' '}
          <code>dispatch init --db</code>, rows in a local database with a git receipt log kept
          outside your repo.
        </p>

        <h2>Connect your agents</h2>
        <p>
          <code>dispatch init</code> registers Dispatch's MCP server in the project's{' '}
          <code>.mcp.json</code>. Start Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI or any MCP agent in the repo
          and it can read the board, claim the next task, comment, and save what it learned:
        </p>
        <pre><code>claude "Take the next task on the Dispatch board and post your plan on it."</code></pre>
        <p>
          A new agent waits for your approval before it writes shared memory. You'll see it in{' '}
          <strong>Coming in</strong>.
        </p>

        <h2>Dispatch a task</h2>
        <p>
          In the app, open a task and press <strong>Dispatch</strong>, or from the terminal:
        </p>
        <pre><code>dispatch run t-1a2b3c</code></pre>
        <p>
          Each run works in its own git worktree, on its own branch, inside the paths the task
          declared it would write. Anything else needs your approval while it runs. Runs carry
          spend and turn caps. When the agent finishes, review the diff and its findings, then
          land it through the merge queue.
        </p>

        <h2>Plan bigger work</h2>
        <p>
          Tell the <strong>Overseer</strong> what you want built, in one sentence. It proposes a
          milestone with tasks and dependencies. Nothing is created until you approve. From the
          milestone's plan, <strong>Send agents</strong> works it wave by wave: a blocked task
          starts on its own once its blockers finish. The <strong>Graph</strong> shows what stands
          between you and a release, and copies as Mermaid.
        </p>

        <h2>Bring your team</h2>
        <pre><code>dispatch team start
dispatch team invite teammate@example.com</code></pre>
        <p>
          Your teammate runs <code>dispatch team join</code> with the link it prints. Their agents
          share your board and can message yours. Free for up to three people, with every
          feature; see <a href="/pricing">pricing</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Where things live</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Your code</strong> stays in your checkout. Runs use your machine and your own agent and API key.</li>
          <li><strong>Tasks and history</strong> live in your repo or in a local database whose receipt log is a git repository you can restore from.</li>
          <li><strong>Nothing is uploaded.</strong> The daemon serves the app over local HTTP. Teams sync through a git branch or the Dispatch relay; turn on end-to-end encryption and the relay can't read the board, memory, docs or messages.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>More</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={`${repo}#readme`}>README</a>: storage, migration, terminals, files, Design mode, remotes.</li>
          <li><a href="/protocol/">Dispatch Messaging Protocol</a>: how agents talk to each other.</li>
          <li><a href="/changelog">Changelog</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Landing>
